256:3dd4cb5056a11eb999c8f29271e95b03797e6936ff8d044c4ec9db088a77e14e","file":"preprints/Approximate-counting-of-common-bases-of-two-matroids-September-23-2026/build/main.tex","line":86,"producer_hash_method":"atlas-whitespace-normalized-text","producer_statement_sha256":"c628e75ea6ee187fe6fb1eb1a196d5871c595adba3b9a33632a0d63149cf5bae","provenance":{"kind":"computed","pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","source":"preprints/Approximate-counting-of-common-bases-of-two-matroids-September-23-2026/build/main.tex"},"sha256":"sha256:3dd4cb5056a11eb999c8f29271e95b03797e6936ff8d044c4ec9db088a77e14e","url":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Approximate-counting-of-common-bases-of-two-matroids-September-23-2026/build/main.tex#L86"},"statement_kind":"theorem","text":"\\label{thm:main}\nThere is a single randomized oracle algorithm with the following\nguarantee.  Given independence oracles for arbitrary rank-$r$ matroids\n$M_1,M_2$ on $[n]$, and rational $\\epsilon,\\delta\\in(0,1)$, it outputs a\nnonnegative rational $\\widehat Z$ such that\n\\[\n \\Prob\\bigl[(1-\\epsilon)Z(M_1,M_2)\\le\\widehat Z\n                  \\le(1+\\epsilon)Z(M_1,M_2)\\bigr]\\ge1-\\delta.\n\\]\nIf $Z(M_1,M_2)=0$, its output is always zero.  On every execution, the\nnumber of oracle calls and the number of other bit operations are bounded\nby a fixed polynomial in\n\\[\n n,\\qquad \\ell,\\qquad \\epsilon^{-1},\\qquad \\log\\delta^{-1},\n\\]\nwhere $\\ell$ is the binary encoding length of $n,r,\\epsilon,\\delta$.\nThe algorithm uses only independent unbiased random bits and does not\nrequire a representation of either matroid."}
